WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:127826

1
00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:03.837
In this lesson, you'll
learn how to use the open source library

2
00:00:03.837 --> 00:00:06.940
Instructor to generate structured
output from model providers

3
00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.943
that don't support
formal structured output.

4
00:00:10.076 --> 00:00:12.145
All right, let's dive into the code.

5
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:13.813
Let's talk about Instructor.

6
00:00:13.813 --> 00:00:18.651
Instructor is an open source re-prompting
based structured output library.

7
00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:22.222
If model output doesn't match the format
we requested, we try again.

8
00:00:22.288 --> 00:00:25.025
Here, we can see
our prompt goes into our language model.

9
00:00:25.025 --> 00:00:26.760
We get some form of output.

10
00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.562
Then we check whether or not it's
valid syntax.

11
00:00:29.562 --> 00:00:31.698
If it is, we return it to the user.

12
00:00:31.698 --> 00:00:36.236
If not, we provide the output
and the error back to the language model

13
00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:39.706
and try to get output again
until we get valid output.

14
00:00:40.106 --> 00:00:42.575
Let's talk about some pros
and cons of Instructor.

15
00:00:42.575 --> 00:00:44.310
Instructor it's simple to use.

16
00:00:44.310 --> 00:00:46.446
It has a wide range of provider support

17
00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:50.884
like Anthropic, OpenAI,
Together.AI, Fireworks.AI etc.

18
00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:54.821
It has consistent APIs across providers
so you can avoid vendor lock in

19
00:00:55.088 --> 00:00:57.924
and it supports everything
that pydantic does.

20
00:00:57.924 --> 00:00:58.925
On the downside,

21
00:00:58.925 --> 00:01:03.329
Instructor can't fully enforce structure
so parsing failures will occur.

22
00:01:03.430 --> 00:01:07.500
Retry is can unexpectedly increase costs
in both money and time.

23
00:01:07.567 --> 00:01:11.337
Every time there's a parsing failure,
you have to resend the entire prompt

24
00:01:11.337 --> 00:01:12.338
back to the model.

25
00:01:12.338 --> 00:01:17.277
Instructor is not easily transparent
in re-prompting and/or retrys used.

26
00:01:17.477 --> 00:01:19.612
You have to kind of
hack into the internals

27
00:01:19.612 --> 00:01:21.915
a little bit to try and understand
what's going on.

28
00:01:21.915 --> 00:01:26.052
And Instructor only works with instruction
tuned models with function calling

29
00:01:26.052 --> 00:01:27.654
or tool use or JSON mode.

30
00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:31.424
Other approaches like Outlines,
can use any model, whether or not

31
00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.026
they're instruction tuned.

32
00:01:33.026 --> 00:01:37.597
So in this lab we're going to demonstrate
some simple use of Instructor

33
00:01:37.664 --> 00:01:41.501
to get structured output from Together.AI
which doesn't support

34
00:01:41.501 --> 00:01:43.136
formal structured generation.

35
00:01:43.136 --> 00:01:45.705
Then we're going to highlight
some limitations of Instructor.

36
00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.708
So you can kind of make some analysis
as to whether it makes sense

37
00:01:48.708 --> 00:01:49.776
in your application.

38
00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:51.711
So let's go to the code.

39
00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:55.081
Let's start by getting our Together
API key.

40
00:01:55.215 --> 00:01:57.650
As long as you're on the DeepLearning.AI
platform,

41
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.352
this key will be available to you.

42
00:01:59.352 --> 00:02:03.323
If you're working on your own platform,
you'll need to use your own API key,

43
00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:06.493
Here's how you connect to the Together
AI server.

44
00:02:06.493 --> 00:02:09.496
We're going to import Instructor
and then OpenAI.

45
00:02:09.529 --> 00:02:14.529
You'll note that we're importing OpenAI
rather than some other Together AI API.

46
00:02:14.934 --> 00:02:17.704
And the reason for this
is that most inference providers

47
00:02:17.704 --> 00:02:22.275
use the OpenAI
standard for language model inference.

48
00:02:22.509 --> 00:02:26.913
And what that means is
you can use the OpenAI SDK by

49
00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:30.783
just changing the base URL to pointing to
whatever your language model provider is.

50
00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:33.953
So when you run this,
you'll pull in your Together client.

51
00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.057
To generate unstructured text
from Together you can use the standard

52
00:02:38.057 --> 00:02:41.861
OpenAI tools chat dot completions
dot create.

53
00:02:42.061 --> 00:02:45.665
Then you provide any model that Together.AI
serves.

54
00:02:45.732 --> 00:02:49.836
In this case we're going to use Llama 3.1
the 8 billion parameter model.

55
00:02:50.036 --> 00:02:52.172
And the instruction tuned model.

56
00:02:52.172 --> 00:02:55.842
If you see instruct in a model name, note
that it has nothing to do

57
00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:56.776
with Instructor.

58
00:02:56.776 --> 00:03:00.480
Instruct inside of a model name just means
that the model has been instruction

59
00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.051
tuned to have chatbot-like functionality
like Claude or ChatGPT.

60
00:03:05.385 --> 00:03:08.888
Next, we're going to provide a user prompt
and we're just going to say "sup"

61
00:03:09.189 --> 00:03:12.425
then, when we run this
we'll see that the language model says,

62
00:03:12.425 --> 00:03:15.395
"sup back at you,
how's your day going so far?"

63
00:03:15.495 --> 00:03:17.497
I would say pretty good robot.

64
00:03:17.497 --> 00:03:22.497
To use Together, all you have to do is
wrap whatever your client is in.

65
00:03:22.702 --> 00:03:25.371
One of Instructors dot from methods.

66
00:03:25.371 --> 00:03:28.074
So, for example, here
we're using Instructor

67
00:03:28.074 --> 00:03:31.878
dot from OpenAI, and then we're passing it
our Together client.

68
00:03:31.945 --> 00:03:34.347
This will create a new client
called Instructor

69
00:03:34.347 --> 00:03:38.418
Client which will use Instructors tools
to get structured output.

70
00:03:38.985 --> 00:03:42.088
To try out Instructor,
let's define a really simple structure.

71
00:03:42.155 --> 00:03:46.159
In this case we're going to define
greeting here which just has a field hello

72
00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:47.360
which is a string.

73
00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:52.360
To use this, we simply call Instructor
client dot chat dot completions dot

74
00:03:52.365 --> 00:03:53.066
create.

75
00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:57.203
As before, we're going to pass
the same model name in the same messages.

76
00:03:57.203 --> 00:03:59.806
So nothing's changed
from the unstructured version.

77
00:03:59.806 --> 00:04:03.476
The only thing that we're adding
is response model equals greeting.

78
00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.979
That's
the class that we expect to receive.

79
00:04:05.979 --> 00:04:09.949
If you're using OpenAI structured output,
you'll note that they use response

80
00:04:09.949 --> 00:04:13.753
format as the keyword argument
rather than Instructor's response model.

81
00:04:13.987 --> 00:04:15.655
So make sure you pay attention to that.

82
00:04:15.655 --> 00:04:16.556
When we run this,

83
00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:20.326
we'll see that we get greeting
with sup inside of the hello field.

84
00:04:20.727 --> 00:04:24.197
Let's write a simple calendar
event extractor to test Instructor out.

85
00:04:24.297 --> 00:04:28.301
So here what we want to do is provide
a rough description of event to the model.

86
00:04:28.301 --> 00:04:31.971
And then get back some kind of structured
output containing the name of the event,

87
00:04:32.272 --> 00:04:35.375
the date that the event is occurring,
a list of participants

88
00:04:35.675 --> 00:04:37.410
where the event is occurring,

89
00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:41.014
as well as some additional address
information like state code and zip code.

90
00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:43.283
You'll note here
that name can be any string.

91
00:04:43.283 --> 00:04:47.253
Inside of calendar event,
we have a date field with a date type

92
00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:50.023
which comes from The Python
standard library.

93
00:04:50.023 --> 00:04:54.460
OpenAI doesn't support the use
of specialized types like this. OpenAI

94
00:04:54.460 --> 00:04:57.497
would require that you have a string
here.

95
00:04:57.563 --> 00:05:00.600
Instructor
is much more general than OpenAI, and

96
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:05.600
so you can use far more of pydantic
typing support than OpenAI supports.

97
00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:10.838
So if you ended up using OpenAI structured
output tooling, you will get an error

98
00:05:10.877 --> 00:05:13.379
if you try to use date,
at least for the moment.

99
00:05:13.379 --> 00:05:17.417
Next, we have a list of participants
and this is a list of person.

100
00:05:17.750 --> 00:05:21.120
And we have class person here
with only a name field.

101
00:05:21.187 --> 00:05:23.189
You'll note here
that there's only a name field.

102
00:05:23.189 --> 00:05:25.658
So why don't we just make this
a list of strings?

103
00:05:25.658 --> 00:05:26.726
Well, the reason for that

104
00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:29.862
is that if you have a person class,
you can easily extend it

105
00:05:29.862 --> 00:05:34.367
later with additional information
like email address as needed.

106
00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:37.470
Just following kind of good software
engineering practices.

107
00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:41.741
Then we have address number, street name,
city name, nothing complicated there.

108
00:05:41.908 --> 00:05:45.545
And then the remaining two have a few
interesting points we need to address.

109
00:05:45.645 --> 00:05:47.980
You'll note here
that these are equals field.

110
00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:50.183
This is a tool inside of pydantic.

111
00:05:50.183 --> 00:05:54.120
You can specify a regular expression
that defines the values

112
00:05:54.120 --> 00:05:56.155
that state code may take.

113
00:05:56.155 --> 00:05:59.892
In this case, we're
requiring that state code be only exactly

114
00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:04.630
two capital letters,
such as OR for Oregon, NY for New York.

115
00:06:04.864 --> 00:06:06.566
These are all U.S. state codes.

116
00:06:06.566 --> 00:06:08.267
This regular expression here

117
00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:11.571
requires that anything inside of zip
code must have five digits.

118
00:06:11.938 --> 00:06:14.941
You'll learn a little bit
more about regular expressions

119
00:06:15.174 --> 00:06:16.676
at the end of the course.

120
00:06:16.676 --> 00:06:18.778
Then let's write an example

121
00:06:18.778 --> 00:06:20.012
event description.

122
00:06:20.012 --> 00:06:23.082
So Alice and Bob are going to a science
fair on Friday.

123
00:06:23.416 --> 00:06:27.220
Science fair is hosted at the gymnasium
of the Hazel Dale Elementary School,

124
00:06:27.353 --> 00:06:29.522
which is my elementary school growing up.

125
00:06:29.522 --> 00:06:32.291
Next, let's write a function
called generate.

126
00:06:32.291 --> 00:06:37.263
And generate is going to accept the format
that we want to return the user prompt

127
00:06:37.330 --> 00:06:40.333
and then the system prompt
if you'd like to change it.

128
00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:44.003
It'll also provide a default model
with eight billion parameter model.

129
00:06:44.237 --> 00:06:48.775
But you can change this to use the 70
billion parameter model if you like.

130
00:06:48.941 --> 00:06:52.111
We can also set max three tries to three,
but we'll talk about that

131
00:06:52.111 --> 00:06:52.979
in just a second.

132
00:06:52.979 --> 00:06:54.247
All this function does is it

133
00:06:54.247 --> 00:06:58.151
makes it a little more convenient
to call the Instructor client.

134
00:06:58.718 --> 00:07:00.753
Now, let's actually generate
a calendar event.

135
00:07:00.753 --> 00:07:02.355
So here we specify system prompt.

136
00:07:02.355 --> 00:07:03.756
We say make a calendar event

137
00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:07.660
respond in JSON with the event name, date,
list of participants, and the address.

138
00:07:07.727 --> 00:07:10.129
Note here
that I've kind of roughly communicated

139
00:07:10.129 --> 00:07:13.065
the structure
that the model is expected to return.

140
00:07:13.065 --> 00:07:16.803
You don't have to tell it exactly what
it's supposed to return the exact like

141
00:07:16.936 --> 00:07:19.972
provide an example of JSON,
but it can help

142
00:07:19.972 --> 00:07:22.608
usually. Anytime
you're requesting structured output,

143
00:07:22.608 --> 00:07:26.479
make sure you kind of communicate
what the model is expected to return.

144
00:07:26.612 --> 00:07:28.047
It's generally best practice.

145
00:07:28.047 --> 00:07:29.482
Then we'll provide a user prompt,

146
00:07:29.482 --> 00:07:33.052
which is just event description
plus whatever event description you wrote.

147
00:07:33.352 --> 00:07:36.722
And then we're going to call our generate
function here to get an event.

148
00:07:36.889 --> 00:07:39.492
Now, let's take a look at the event
that we got back.

149
00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:42.495
We can see here
we got calendar event, name science fair.

150
00:07:42.595 --> 00:07:46.666
We got dates, participants
with the list of people, the address

151
00:07:46.666 --> 00:07:51.003
number, the street name, the city
name, the state code, and the zip code.

152
00:07:51.170 --> 00:07:54.507
You'll notice here the zip code is 97005.

153
00:07:54.507 --> 00:07:57.410
Even though we didn't actually specify it
inside of the prompt.

154
00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:01.681
This is an example of how you can elicit
additional information from your model

155
00:08:01.681 --> 00:08:02.915
by providing structure.

156
00:08:02.915 --> 00:08:07.286
The model was actually able to guess
the zip code within a rough distance.

157
00:08:07.286 --> 00:08:10.289
The correct zip code here is 97005.

158
00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:13.559
If you change the model
to use the 70 billion parameter model,

159
00:08:13.759 --> 00:08:17.864
this should probably get the exact right
zip code of 97007,

160
00:08:17.864 --> 00:08:19.799
even though it's not provided
in the prompt.

161
00:08:19.799 --> 00:08:23.536
Why don't you play around with this
a little bit by going to change the event

162
00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:26.572
description, see
what else you can get the model print out.

163
00:08:27.273 --> 00:08:30.309
Let's talk about retries.
Instructor can use more tokens

164
00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:32.078
than other forms of structured output.

165
00:08:32.078 --> 00:08:34.981
So when an output fails to validate,
Instructor will provide feedback

166
00:08:34.981 --> 00:08:38.551
to the model or request new output
until the response parses correctly.

167
00:08:38.885 --> 00:08:42.588
Instructor doesn't make it super easy
to track the number of retries

168
00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:46.058
and the number of tokens
used across retries, so, I've provided

169
00:08:46.058 --> 00:08:50.029
some utility functions here
to track retries and token usage.

170
00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:53.599
Let's extract the same calendar event
to see how many attempts it took.

171
00:08:53.866 --> 00:08:55.868
And we're going to clear the tracker here.

172
00:08:55.868 --> 00:09:00.540
So then we make sure we're not tracking
retries across multiple completions.

173
00:09:01.173 --> 00:09:04.143
We run this we see that
we get the exact same calendar event.

174
00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:07.179
But let's check the number of tokens
and retry as used. Here,

175
00:09:07.179 --> 00:09:12.179
we see that there was only one retry used
671 input tokens and 85 output tokens.

176
00:09:13.386 --> 00:09:16.222
Great. Instructor was able to get the model
to produce

177
00:09:16.222 --> 00:09:19.225
the output correctly
without any retry as required.

178
00:09:19.592 --> 00:09:22.962
In cases where the schema is complicated
or the prompting is insufficient,

179
00:09:23.262 --> 00:09:25.565
the model output will require
multiple retries

180
00:09:25.565 --> 00:09:28.200
and possibly
never generate the correct output.

181
00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:31.203
To see this, let's define
a complicated class.

182
00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:34.373
It's not actually that complicated,
but it will be annoying for the model

183
00:09:34.373 --> 00:09:35.341
to try to generate.

184
00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:38.678
We're going to add just an A field,
which has to be cat,

185
00:09:38.678 --> 00:09:42.348
dog or hat, an integer
and a boolean with A, B and C.

186
00:09:42.448 --> 00:09:46.452
Next, we're going to try and break
Instructor to demonstrate retries.

187
00:09:46.586 --> 00:09:48.554
So this might not reflect common usage.

188
00:09:48.554 --> 00:09:51.357
But you should note that parsing failures
will happen by chance

189
00:09:51.357 --> 00:09:55.061
if you're performing hundreds
or thousands of language model calls. Here,

190
00:09:55.161 --> 00:09:58.164
we've requested the complicated class.

191
00:09:58.431 --> 00:10:01.767
We have a user prompt here
that says, please write me a short

192
00:10:01.767 --> 00:10:05.137
essay on Dolly Parton, and then the system
prompt don't give me what I want.

193
00:10:05.438 --> 00:10:08.107
Note in here, we're actually
shooting ourselves in the foot.

194
00:10:08.107 --> 00:10:10.876
We haven't communicated the structure
of complicated to the model,

195
00:10:10.876 --> 00:10:13.879
and we're intentionally
asking it to sabotage us.

196
00:10:14.080 --> 00:10:17.183
We're limiting Instructor
to three maximum tries here.

197
00:10:18.050 --> 00:10:20.753
And here we see that it failed to parse.

198
00:10:20.753 --> 00:10:22.788
We give our model an opportunity
to produce

199
00:10:22.788 --> 00:10:26.192
a correct output three times,
and it failed all three times.

200
00:10:26.559 --> 00:10:29.695
And this was pretty costly for us
because we had to triple our token

201
00:10:29.695 --> 00:10:32.698
usage, didn't get the correct output,
and still had to wait.

202
00:10:32.865 --> 00:10:36.802
Here, we see that we have 383 input
tokens used three times,

203
00:10:37.103 --> 00:10:41.741
and three output tokens that presumably
contain short essays about Dolly Parton.

204
00:10:41.841 --> 00:10:46.112
We use a total of 2600 tokens,
and it required three retries.

205
00:10:46.278 --> 00:10:49.315
You can imagine this gets very costly
for much larger prompts

206
00:10:49.315 --> 00:10:52.318
in the order of thousands
or tens of thousands of tokens.

207
00:10:52.585 --> 00:10:55.087
So why don't you take a shot
at fixing this?

208
00:10:55.087 --> 00:10:56.288
Look at this code here.

209
00:10:56.288 --> 00:10:58.057
We're doing the exact same thing.

210
00:10:58.057 --> 00:11:01.594
All you need to do is modify
the user prompt in the system prompt

211
00:11:01.927 --> 00:11:03.863
so that we get the correct output.

212
00:11:03.863 --> 00:11:04.664
Take a shot at that

213
00:11:04.664 --> 00:11:07.900
now. So, hopefully you got a chance
to play around with this a little bit.

214
00:11:08.434 --> 00:11:11.771
I was able to fix this by saying,
give me A, B and C,

215
00:11:12.071 --> 00:11:14.240
I didn't communicate
too much about the structure.

216
00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:16.208
And then I said, just give me what I want.

217
00:11:16.208 --> 00:11:17.543
Nothing complicated.

218
00:11:17.543 --> 00:11:20.212
And when we run this,
we see we get A equals cat,

219
00:11:20.212 --> 00:11:23.215
B equals one, C equals true.

220
00:11:23.315 --> 00:11:26.285
Lastly, we can see that
we did this pretty efficiently.

221
00:11:26.285 --> 00:11:28.921
We required only one retry.

222
00:11:28.921 --> 00:11:31.090
In this lesson,
you learned how to use Instructor

223
00:11:31.090 --> 00:11:33.526
to get structured output
from any model provider

224
00:11:33.526 --> 00:11:35.761
and some possible
limitations of Instructor

225
00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:38.364
that you should consider
when you're building your software.

226
00:11:38.364 --> 00:11:40.866
In the next lesson,
you'll go under the hood of outlines

227
00:11:40.866 --> 00:11:44.036
to see how structured generation
is able to quickly generate

228
00:11:44.036 --> 00:11:46.939
reliable, structured
output with no retry as required.

